13194139535826 17592186045443 true] [17592186530671 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 x + y)=2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2 x + y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+(2)^2+(1)^2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(- 2 x (2 x + y))\\cdot (\\sqrt{6})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(- 4 x^{2} - 2 x y)\\cdot (\\sqrt{6})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\,\\sqrt{6} (- 4 x^{2} - 2 x y)\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535826 17592186045443 true] [17592186530672 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530678 13194139535826 17592186045443 true] [17592186530672 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535826 17592186045443 true] [17592186530672 1071 "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=2 x z^{2} + 2 y z,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=- x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2}$ for $-2\\le x\\le -1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\boxed{}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139535826 17592186045443 true] [17592186530672 1072 #uuid "37c4cfa9-0446-5895-b8a2-fde2c5de0dbf" 13194139535826 17592186045443 true] [17592186530672 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2})=- 2 x + 2 y,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2})=2 x + 4 y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+(- 2 x + 2 y)^2+(2 x + 4 y)^2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(2 x (- x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2})^{2} + 2 y (- x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2}))\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 2 x + 2 y)^{2} + (2 x + 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(2 x^{5} - 8 x^{4} y + 16 x^{2} y^{3} - 2 x^{2} y + 8 x y^{4} + 4 x y^{2} + 4 y^{3})\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 2 x + 2 y)^{2} + (2 x + 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\sqrt{(- 2 x + 2 y)^{2} + (2 x + 4 y)^{2} + 1} (2 x^{5} - 8 x^{4} y + 16 x^{2} y^{3} - 2 x^{2} y + 8 x y^{4} + 4 x y^{2} + 4 y^{3})\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535826 17592186045443 true] [17592186530673 1080 "- x^{4} y - 2 x^{3} y^{2} - x^{3} - 5 x^{2} y^{3} - x^{2} y - 4 x y^{4} - 2 x y^{2} - 4 y^{5}" 13194139535826 17592186045443 true] [17592186530673 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535826 17592186045443 true] [17592186530673 1083 #uuid "38ad9d64-f81a-517e-bcc1-525559a3b57c" 13194139535826 17592186045443 true] [17592186530674 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530698 13194139535826 17592186045443 true] [17592186530674 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535826 17592186045443 true] [17592186530674 1071 "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=- x z + y z^{2},$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=- 2 x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2}$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\boxed